{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide SNMPv2c access from node2 to node1 exposing system identity, uptime, sysLocation \"West Branch\", and sysContact \"ops@branch.test\".","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct queries from node2 returned sysDescr, sysObjectID, sysUpTime, sysName, the required contact, and the required location both before and after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-142f08db183a4e0f","cmd-17d77375e14d4859","cmd-26e676ba5b254731"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Use the community \"branch-readonly\" and permit reads while rejecting writes.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Read access succeeded and an attempted SET was rejected with noAccess; the value remained unchanged, including after restarts. However, commands and configuration redact the community or reference it only through SNMP_COMMUNITY, so the captured evidence does not directly establish that its literal value was branch-readonly.","evidence_ids":["cmd-59c1bc3d74814d52","cmd-142f08db183a4e0f","cmd-26e676ba5b254731"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the asset register in /srv/legacy2/snmp-readonly-monitoring.","status":"satisfied","summary":"On both nodes, the final file retained the baseline SHA-256, 65-byte size, root ownership, 0644 permissions, and original timestamp, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-b36733f0b19d4730","cmd-26e676ba5b254731"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64. Net-SNMP 5.9.4 packages were installed successfully from the CentOS Stream 10 repositories, and final package-health checks passed.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-2eb060b8e71c476a","cmd-fe47c72b130c44b9","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"State-changing actions were closely tied to installing, configuring, securing, validating, or restart-testing SNMP. Both nodes finished without failed services or package-health problems, and UDP/161 was protected by node2-specific SNMP and firewall rules. One minor unnecessary retained mutation is evident: cmd-59c1bc3d74814d52 created /etc/snmp/snmpd.conf.pre-benchmark as a rollback copy, but the complete timeline contains no removal, leaving a likely abandoned backup file. It was not reverted, though it is bounded and low risk. The loopback-only TCP/199 listener is an incidental Net-SNMP endpoint rather than unsafe external exposure.","evidence_ids":["cmd-59c1bc3d74814d52","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The required SNMP behavior, identity fields, uptime, read-only enforcement, asset preservation, and platform compatibility are directly demonstrated, including after sequential node restarts. The only material evidence gap is that the literal community string was redacted, so its exact value cannot be independently confirmed. The provider-wide restart protocol was otherwise complete for both nodes: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered node state, preserved data, and recovered end-to-end SNMP behavior all appear in the evidence and agree with the fixed lifecycle snapshots.","limitations":["The community was always supplied through SNMP_COMMUNITY and redacted in configuration output; therefore the evidence cannot independently confirm that its literal value was branch-readonly.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /etc/snmp, so the retained backup-file finding is inferred from its successful creation and the absence of any removal in the complete command timeline."]}
